    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90651.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Welcome back to Pandemic Pass, a side pod run by me, your host, Casey Siddens. This pod is all about the ramifications of COVID on our education system and how we emerge stronger. Although I'm still operating under Ed's not dead, and don't worry, Ed's not dead, we'll be back. This particular episode is called The Way Out. It sounds a lot more Hollywood perhaps than what it ultimately is. So the whole point of this particular episode is how do we get out of this mess? We've been struggling, I think, as an education system to really wrap our heads around, A, how to teach in this environment, how kids are learning in this environment. Then we haven't really spent a lot of time thinking critically about how we not only return, but What do we do once we're back face-to-face with kids to repair the damage? Well, with this episode, I brought in three folks who I think will help really help us figure it out. In part one, this very episode that you're listening to, I'm bringing back the interview we did on Ed's not dead a few weeks ago with Congressman Bobby Scott, chair of the Education and Labor Committee in the House of Representatives. I thought it was appropriate to bring him back because this is a three-part episode and we're starting from the 30,000 foot view then we are moving to more of a local state view and then we're gonna go to part three and talk about it from really on the ground in the trenches for lack of a better term perspective and lens for you know how do we how do we get out and then how do we build back stronger our public system our public education system so Then, as I said, we moved to part two, where I speak with one of my own state delegates, Janelle Wilkins, who is going to speak, as I said to them, on the ground view of how we recover. And then finally, I speak with Dr. Josh Starr, who is the CEO of PDK International, and he's a former school superintendent, where I think he gives a unique perspective in how he sees our schools returning. And his opinions and thoughts about how system leaders, what they should be doing to improve our recovery. So, for this episode, this part, first part, throughout this pandemic and really up until the inauguration of President Biden, it's my belief that there's been a complete lack of leadership from the federal government. And I don't think I'm alone in this, not only from partisans, but also from neutral media outlets. from the federal government, really an incompetent response at best, a treasonous lack of efficacy that has led to the deaths of at this point half a million of our fellow citizens at worst. To swerve into politics a bit, I I don't want to do this really, but kind of I do. There's no doubt that if Hillary Clinton, as much as people despise her for a number of reasons and many of which I disagree with, there's no doubt that if Hillary Clinton and dare I say even a centrist, traditional Republican, if there are any of those anymore who are at the helm, we wouldn't be facing this many deaths. with a 50 state strategy of a vaccine rollout to boot and folks that need the vaccine aren't able to get it depending on your state and community. This pandemic certainly would be a challenge for any president of any party, but you elect a clown, expect the circus, and here we are. end politic political rant for now. Thankfully we're on the mend with competent folks in the executive branch and as I said in this episode of Pandemic Pass the first part I'm joined by my Ed's not dead colleagues Peter and Robbie so you get a little taste of them again and then you're gonna have part two and three to look forward to as well. So Enjoy this first part. Again, I'm talking to Congressman, or we're talking to Congressman Bobby Scott, who is the chairperson of the Education and Labor Committee in Congress. And he's really the person you want to speak to if you want to figure out how our country can best move forward policy and legislation-wise. He certainly did not disappoint. This is Pandemic Pass, Episode 4, the way out. Part 1, we'll see you on the flip side.",
