    "turn_text": "disinfected and PPE you've got to pay for. That costs more money. And ventilation, which is a huge problem with the schools, with the ages of the schools. And the fact that a lot of schools have eating ventilation, air conditioning problems, preexisting, We need to make sure they have proper ventilation. If you're in a school without proper ventilation, if one student is infected by the end of the day, they've all got it. If you've got proper ventilation, you have a chance of surviving. And so proper ventilation and fixing the HVAC systems and all of these schools is expensive because a lot of them have problems. So we need the resources and that's why it's so important that in the COVID relief bills, we have money for education. One of the disappointments in the last relief bill that we've just completed work on is the fact that they don't have any money for state and local government. With the reduction in revenue, the only way they can state and local government can balance their budgets, which they have to, is either fire people or cancel contracts and let the contractor fire people. I mean, when you get to education, which is a huge portion of local and state government, they have to lay off education officials, about 700 and some thousand education officials across the country have been laid off already. Those are people who will not be there for instructional purposes. So the fact that we didn't do state and local is a real disappointment. It means that the money we have in their education will, instead of helping the situation, we'll just go to offset some of the cuts.",
