  {
    "episode_id": 90651,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90651.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, we have to refocus on the achievement gap. That's the number one priority. Another thing is we have to deal with the segregation of schools across the country. J.O. report a couple of years ago found that the schools today are as segregated today as they were in the 1960s. And getting worse. I mean, how is that possible? Yeah. And we have found that integrated schools, integrated schools do much better for all of the students. Achievement gaps are much less. The academic achievement is much better. And so there is a compelling reason to try to integrate the schools. Before you get to the social reasons and just basic civil rights, it's a better way to educate the students. And there are a lot of things that we can do. Two things the House passed that the Senate hasn't taken up. One is to codify a program that Obama started right at the end of his term, a little grant program, to help localities who want to voluntarily integrate their schools come up with a plan that's effective. There's a little challenge there because in Seattle and in Louisville, Kentucky, they had voluntary plans and the Supreme Court found they were unconstitutional. Now you can do it, but you got to do it right. And so you need a little technical assistance and that costs a little money. So you can apply for this little grant program to get some technical assistance so you can fashion an effective a plan that can withstand a constitutional challenge. I said it was at the end of his administration, several localities applied for the money, but before any money went out, the new administration came in and canceled the program. Well, we want to codify that program, and we passed legislation to do it, but the Senate hadn't taken it up. The other is the Equity and Inclusion Enforcement Act. which would give essentially what's called a private right of action under Title VI. Right now, because of Supreme Court decisions, if you're going to complain about discrimination based on patterns and practices or disparate impact in education, the Department of Education has to come forward and make the",
