  {
    "episode_id": 90651,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90651.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "If they don't do anything, nothing gets done. But what's wrong with, if you feel you've been discriminated against, what's wrong with you being able to bring the case yourself? The Equity and Inclusion Enforcement Act would allow the local civil rights groups, the local parent groups, to come forward and challenge practices that have a discriminatory impact. It also sets up an Assistant Secretary within the Department of Education and monitors like we have on the Title IX so that there would be somebody in the local education, in the local education, in its local school system, whose job it is to make sure that nobody's discriminating. That bill has passed the House and had been taken up by the Senate. We need to pass that. But there is one thing that we have been successful in doing, and that is to remove about a 40-year-old vestige of the pre-1954 days. And that is a provision in law that prevents the use of federal funds and transportation for the purpose of inter-school integration. That has been the law. You cannot use federal funds and transportation for the purpose of integrating your schools. That's been the law. And finally, by virtue of the COVID relief bill, we're able to get rid of that provision. And so you can use federal funds if you need to, if you need to spend a little money on transportation to integrate the schools. And some of it is federal money. You can do it.",
